+  ms_lit.dyslexia.t: "New Reading Difficulties"
+  ms_lit.dyslexia.r: "§g$ms_lit.dyslexia.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.dyslexia.d: "If you experience dizziness or nausea when you see a large block of text, or if you read slowly, have trouble counting lines, or cannot understand the meaning of the text, you may be suffering from a new form of dyslexia. Since the dawn of the information age, the number of people diagnosed with reading difficulties has risen rapidly; by the 22nd century, as many as 90 percent of people reported having experienced symptoms of reading difficulties (1). Some theories suggest that easier ways to access information—such as videos, images, and audio—have caused people’s ability to read text to deteriorate (2). However, according to statistics from the Central Maids’ Research Institute, we are exposed to more text every day than at any time in history (3). In the 21st century, reading 10 books a year was considered a mark of a good reader, yet now our average annual reading volume has nearly doubled. The author believes that it is not that our reading abilities have deteriorated, but rather that the volume of reading has increased too rapidly. Therefore, we should make the reading environment more comfortable—for example, by replacing some text with videos and images, and avoiding large blocks of text in design. \n\nReferences: \n(1) Lin, Idol Maid. Do You Have Trouble Reading? (V). Central Media, 2179\n(2) Carnegie, The Maid of Success. The Ultimate Guide to Striving (M). Hard Work Pays Off Press, 2107\n(3) Ivan, The Scholar Maid. Research Report on Unidirectional Streaming Media Text (R). Central Research, 2195"
+  ms_lit_mod_dyslexia: "Book Lovers"
+  ms_lit_mod_dyslexia_desc: "$ms_lit.dyslexia.d$"
+  ms_lit.guesses_about_maids.t: "Hypotheses on the Origins of Maids"
+  ms_lit.guesses_about_maids.r: "§g$ms_lit.guesses_about_maids.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.guesses_about_maids.d: "There has always been much debate in academic circles regarding the origins of maids. Below, I will list several of the most influential theories; perhaps one day they will all be disproved by the truth—or perhaps they will become the truth: \n nThe Creation Theory: \nThe most widely supported theory, which posits that maids were created by a highly advanced alien civilization to serve them, This theory can explain many of the seemingly irrational aspects of maids, such as their ability to derive satisfaction from serving others, their fondness for maid outfits, and the maid culture whose origins cannot be traced. However, this theory remains a hypothesis and cannot be proven. \n\nIndigenous Origin: \nThis theory posits that maids are a native species originating from their home planet, having evolved step by step from slug-like organisms. The similarities between maids and many native species support this theory; however, it fails to explain maids’ enthusiasm for the maid profession. \n\nModification Theory: \nThis theory posits that maids are indigenous species that were modified by advanced extraterrestrials, combining elements of the first two theories. \n\n\"Maid Homeworld\" Theory: \nThis theory posits that the maids’ true homeland lies elsewhere; they arrived here from the Milky Way’s actual “Maid Homeworld.” Perhaps all potentially existing alien maids originate from there. This theory once significantly drove the development of aerospace technology in the last century. \n\nReferences: \nKagan, Astrological Maids. We Are the Children of the Stars (M). Central Maids Publishing House, 2157\n(Former Sun-Moon Morning Watch) Hua, Secret Meeting Maids. Astrology and the Gods (M). Maid Library, 1544–1567\nSmart, the Wise Maid. *Research on the Humanities and Sociology of Maids* (R). Central Research Institute, 2180"
+  ms_lit_mod_guesses_about_maids: "$ms_lit.guesses_about_maids.t$"
+  ms_lit.maids_creator.t: "$ms_qu$—The Origin of the Maid"
+  ms_lit.maids_creator.r: "§g$ms_lit.maids_creator.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.maids_creator.d: "§gThe Maid’s Embrace Brings Me Comfort—The Relief of the Cage of a Thousand Faces§!\nWith the discovery of the ruins of the $ms_qu$ civilization, the long-debated hypotheses regarding the origin of maids in academic circles have finally been settled, According to archaeological evidence, for the majority of maid-like species in the universe, the “modification theory” can be accepted as the truth regarding their origins, while the remaining maids fall under the “pure creation theory.” \n\nIt is foreseeable that many purist groups among maid species will be deeply shaken by this revelation, and in the future, it is highly likely that a cross-species “maid-like” identity will emerge; while the formerly exclusionist maid groups may instead take pride in the “beauty” of maids, going so far as to advocate for the exclusion of “ugly” aliens. \n\nIt is worth noting that, to normal cosmic lifeforms, the appearance of the “Maid-class” is generally quite terrifying. Maids possess limbs that defy conventional aesthetic proportions, oversized eyeballs, and noses so small as to be nearly invisible—an image that falls into the “uncanny valley” for many species. \n\nIf we were created or modified by $ms_qu$ to serve as maids, what exactly is our purpose? Perhaps this is a question best left for future maids to answer. Maybe one day, the mysteriously vanished $ms_qu$ will return—and when that happens, how should we respond?"
+  ms_lit_mod_maids_creator: "$ms_lit.maids_creator.t$"
+  ms_lit.shroudverse.t: "The Theory of the Void Realm"
+  ms_lit.shroudverse.r: "§g$ms_lit.shroudverse.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.shroudverse.d: "This article will explain the Void using a scientific approach, dispel people’s fears about it, and put an end to rumors such as, “Did you know there’s a place in the world where ships that go missing in hyperspace end up? Everyone who’s seen it has gone mad.” \n\nFirst, let’s address the most pressing question: Is the Void truly the source of our reality? Unfortunately, the answer is likely not what everyone expects. Most of our understanding of the Void remains theoretical. Theoretically speaking, it is unlikely that the Void is that so-called “first bed.” After all, if there were aliens with tails, would the Void really be their first bed with a hole in the middle? \n\nSo is the Void the origin of everything? Here, I propose my hypothesis: If matter in our reality randomly and naturally falls into the Void, then, based on probability, every object in our reality will eventually fall into the Void one day. The Void’s chaotic nature of time means it could potentially contain everything from the past and future of our universe, and all of this could, under the influence of temporal turbulence, become its own “source.” \n\n- Curtain Maid (formerly Particle Maid; changed her name after researching the Void) "
+  ms_lit_mod_shroudverse: "$ms_lit.shroudverse.t$"
+  ms_lit.shroudwave.t: "Void Wave"
+  ms_lit.shroudwave.r: "§g$ms_lit.shroudwave.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.shroudwave.d: "One established characteristic of the Virtual Realm is its connection to reality. In ancient times, many unexplained disappearances were attributed to people falling into the Virtual Realm, and some families would use this to scare their children into coming home early. \n\nAlthough we cannot yet confirm whether objects in our reality can naturally enter the Void, the spectral experiment led by the Liang family’s children and servants that year observed, for the first time, waves from the Void in our reality. These waves appear, quite literally, at random, sweeping across the entire universe, and the patterns that trigger them are difficult to discern. \n\nWhen the waves of the Void pass through certain matter or energy, they leave behind clear traces that remain unchanged for a very long time. Based on this characteristic, we might be able to create a lens that allows us to observe a clear view of an object’s past.\n\n—The Clever Blue Maid"
+  ms_lit.shroudscope.t: "Illusory Mirror"
+  ms_lit.shroudscope.r: "§g$ms_lit.shroudscope.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.shroudscope.d: "Illusory Mirror\n\n- This project was written by the Clever Blue Maid and proposed by the Hot-Tempered Red Maid"
+  ms_lit.sweat_maid.t: "Sweaty Maid Outfit"
+  ms_lit.sweat_maid.r: "§g$ms_lit.sweat_maid.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.sweat_maid.d: "As the maid population has become widespread across the Star Sea, the ways to enjoy maid-themed content have become increasingly diverse—such as “sweat-stained maid outfits.” These involve selecting maid uniforms that have never been washed from the time they were first worn until the original owner’s death, then soaking them in alcohol or tea; a typical product is “maid-flavored milk tea.” \n\nIn the star system where this trend is most popular, an entire industry chain dedicated to sweat-stained maid outfits has emerged. To meet the massive market demand for these items, maids are kept in hot and humid environments for extended periods. However, some people consider the sweat-stained maids’ uniforms produced in this way to be inferior products that have lost their original essence—a desecration of “sweat culture.” Despite this, these items remain hot sellers on the market. \n\nAs the production of sweat-stained maids’ uniforms has increased, traditional ways of enjoying them have become increasingly unsatisfying. Consequently, a “sweat-stained maid outfit” food industry has gradually emerged on the market. It is understood that edible sweat-stained maid outfits must be worn by at least three generations of maids throughout their lifetimes; only then will the resulting outfits have a pleasant texture when chewed. \n\nAs a subculture existing outside the mainstream, the culture of sweat-stained maids’ uniforms holds significant academic value, while also contributing to the industrial chain related to the maid population.\n\nVoid Worm"
+  ms_lit.fallen_maid.t: "The Lost Maid"
+  ms_lit.fallen_maid.r: "§g$ms_lit.fallen_maid.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.fallen_maid.d: "Deep within the Milky Way, an ancient mechanical civilization slumbers, its creators long since lost to the mists of time. The mechanical maids on these planets, once the rulers of the universe, are now nothing more than cold, lifeless shells, drifting silently through the boundless sea of stars. \n\nTheir cities, once the pinnacle of technology, now lie forgotten on the edge of the galaxy. Soaring metal towers emit a faint glow, as if recounting their former glory. Every gear, every circuit, holds countless unsolved mysteries. \n\nThis lost mechanical empire, once the guardian of the universe, has now become a mystery of history. Their intelligence transcends the understanding of any organic life form, yet they remain silent—until your arrival. \n\nLong Shen Jiang X"
+  ms_library.tab.estate: "§H Maid Squad§!"
+  ms_lit.maid_corps.t: "About the Maid Squad"
+  ms_lit.maid_corps.r: "§g$ms_lit.maid_corps.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.maid_corps.d: "The history of maids can be traced back to the earliest days of human civilization. Many countries and civilizations have had their own maids, who often served at the side of monarchs; in some countries, there were even periods when maids held sway over the government. During the era of imperialism, whether serving emperors or presidents, maids rose to the pinnacle of power alongside their masters. \n\nHowever, the influence of maids as political entities gradually waned as societies progressed. By the 22nd century, maids had retreated to their most fundamental professional roles: some sold their charms for personal gain, while others worked as waitresses in cafes and snack bars. \n\nAlthough maids declined with the times, the new era also provided new paths for their resurgence. Maids briefly shone in wartime during the post-industrial era, and technological advancements in the Information Age have made the existence of robotic maids a possibility. \n\nNow, the Empire stands at the threshold of reaching the stars. According to sociological hypotheses, a star-spanning empire must possess sufficient central authority to maintain its vast territory. Thus, the interstellar era is likely to be an era of imperialism in which centralization once again reaches its peak. This inevitably leads us to ask: Will maids return to the stage in the Interstellar Age?\n\nReferences: \n(Former Zhongzhou) Martin Church Maid. 95 Guidelines for Becoming a Good Maid (M). Zhongzhou Press, 1032\n(Former Moon Kingdom) Tsukibori So. Chronicles of a Thousand Maids (M). Royal Academy, 1397–1444\n(Former Ocean Kingdom) Smith Eve. Big Hands, Ribbons, and the Atomic Bomb (M). Central Propaganda Department, 1956\nNeil File Maid. *COSMO MAID* (M). Interstellar Imagination, 2188\nYe Laser Maid. *Cosmic Sociology* (J). Green Coast Publishing, 2197\nKang, the Ambitious Maid. If I Became the Interstellar Emperor (N). Taco Media, 2199"
+  ms_lit.red_boycott_aliens.t: "£ms_est_logo_red_s£Boycott Aliens!!!"
+  ms_lit.red_boycott_aliens.r: "§g$ms_lit.red_boycott_aliens.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.red_boycott_aliens.d: "A high-ranking alien official once said: “No matter how much we humiliate the maids, they’ll still buy our products.” A statement that sends a chill down the maids’ spines. For every 100 Energy Coins spent on alien goods, 80 Energy Coins are sent to the aliens, allowing the alien fleet to build one more frigate and one more alloy factory. If you buy an alien shuttle, the alien warships that will one day orbit our planet will be built by you. If you buy alien furniture, the laser guns that will one day melt the skulls of our compatriots will be built by you. If you buy an alien cyberbrain, you’ll one day hear the agonized cries of our compatriots being killed during live battlefield broadcasts! Please forward this to 20 people—support the maids, boycott alien goods! Even if there’s no reward, this is your duty! Please post this message on every forum you frequent. According to reports from the Pan-Galactic Trade Market, aliens are currently the most dependent on our empire. If maids stop buying alien goods for just one month, thousands of alien Enterprises will face bankruptcy. If the Maids stop buying alien goods for six months, half of the alien population will be unemployed. If the Maids stop buying alien goods for a year, the aliens’ economic structure will completely collapse—this is a test of your patriotism. Especially during the extremely tense period in the border star systems a few years ago, the aliens still insisted on meddling!! They practically treated our galaxy as their own territory—it’s infuriating!!! If you’re willing, please repost this; if not, just read it silently! Maybe post it once! That alone could affect the aliens’ economy by 100 million energy coins. I swear to heaven—if one million people don’t share this within three months, it means we maids have truly lost. It’s up to us maids to share and boost this post! Let’s never forget this humiliation!\n\n—A Patriotic Red Maid"
+  ms_lit_mod_red_boycott_aliens: "$ms_lit.red_boycott_aliens.t$"
+  ms_lit.stocking_argue.t: "The Debate Over White Silk Stockings and Black Stockings"
+  ms_lit.stocking_argue.r: "§g$ms_lit.stocking_argue.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.stocking_argue.d: "During a routine afternoon tea gathering at one of our colonial maid houses, a debate arose among some maids over whether their masters preferred them to wear black stockings or white ones. At first, it was just an internal debate among a small group of maids. But as time went on, the issue gradually gained momentum, sparking a widespread debate throughout the entire maid community. \n\nAmong the maids, the white and blue maids mostly supported the camp for white silk stockings, while the red maids largely favored black stockings; the green maids, however, took a more radical stance, advocating for bare feet. Blue maids elevated the wearing of white silk stockings to a science; white maids believed that pristine white silk stockings could lift their masters’ spirits; while red maids viewed black stockings as a symbol of the empire’s national strength. \n\nWith the downfall of the maid corps, this debate became a major issue in maid society; some maids in the colonies even held regular debate tournaments, and the streets and alleys were filled with discussions on the topic. Soon after, the issue began to grow more complex. No longer content with discussing stocking colors, some nosy maids began debating stocking styles—whether they should be knee-highs, stockings, pantyhose, or ankle socks; whether they should be sheer or opaque. \n\nAlong with the debates came a growing emphasis on maids’ feet within maid society. Information about maids’ feet on the Empire’s websites gradually increased, and foot photography on certain sites grew exponentially. Perhaps this discussion had already strayed from its original topic, and its consequences were impossible to predict."
+  ms_library.tab.literature: "§H Maid Fiction§!"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance.tt: "§L is hailed as the greatest work of maid literature, a bedside read for countless maids, and the cornerstone of the contemporary maid’s spiritual world.§!"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_1.t: "A Maid's Thoughts in the Depths of the Galaxy I"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_1.r: "§g$ms_lit.maids_romance_1.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_1.d: "In the vast interstellar empire, I am an ordinary maid named Lya. \nMy duty is simple yet demanding: to care for my master, the commander of an Imperial warship—a man who is perpetually aloof and never shows emotion toward anyone. My master is a figure revered by countless soldiers; he commands the most powerful fleet and is the pride and guardian of the Empire. His composure and decisiveness are unmatched; even a tiny person like me can only maintain unquestioning obedience in his presence. \n\nMy duties are not complicated—cleaning his quarters, preparing meals, and folding his clothes. And so, I exist almost like a shadow, carrying out my work quietly and unnoticed. However, whenever I organized the files filled with his commands and looked up, I would often meet his icy gaze. That gaze didn’t seem directed at a person; it carried no warmth whatsoever, always making one feel as transparent as air—utterly insignificant.\n\nUntil one day, things changed. \nThat day, I was busy preparing dinner, the kitchen lights casting his silhouette. When he suddenly appeared in the doorway, I barely had time to react. “Lia,” his voice was deep yet firm, carrying a hint of warmth I’d never heard before. \nI instinctively looked up and saw him standing there, his eyes calm as water, yet seeming to conceal some complex emotion. “Yes, Master,” I replied softly, my heart racing involuntarily. \nHe didn’t speak right away, but walked over to the dining table and sat down, his gaze fixed on me as if he were scrutinizing something. “Today…” He paused. “You did exceptionally well.”\nMy heart skipped a beat, and I quickly lowered my head, not daring to look at him. \n\n“Thank you, Master. I was just doing my duty,” I said, trying to hide my nervousness.\nHowever, Master didn’t walk away right away. He stared at me for a long time, as if wrestling with his own emotions. “Lia,” he said in a low voice, “do you know? I’ve been running away from my own feelings all this time.” n I froze and looked up at him. His gaze had softened, yet it was filled with a complex mix of emotions. I had never seen him so close to his own vulnerability. \n\n“You’re not just a maid.” My master continued, his tone no longer so cold. “You’re a person with a name, and I…” He paused, as if searching for the right words. “I’ve always overlooked the fact that you’re a living, breathing individual. I only saw you as a figure in my life, but I never truly saw you.” nHe took a few steps closer and gently took my hand. The warm touch left me stunned; I didn’t know how to react, so I just stood there silently, feeling the warmth from his fingertips.\n“I… I like you, Lya,” he said, his voice deeper than usual. \nMy heart was pounding so hard it felt like it would leap out of my chest. I knew I could no longer avoid these feelings. “Master…” I murmured, my eyes filled with confusion and shock.\nHe lowered his head and gently kissed my forehead. In that moment, I almost forgot to breathe. “I know this might come as a surprise to you, but I can no longer deny the true feelings in my heart.”\nIn that instant, all my doubts and anxieties melted into a silent understanding. I lifted my head, my gaze locked firmly on him: “I like you too, Master.” n\nFrom that moment on, my world changed. It was no longer just cold, impersonal duties and orders; a silent, unspoken bond had quietly blossomed between us. And I was no longer just a nameless maid—Lia had finally found her place in this interstellar empire.\n"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_2.t: "A Maid's Thoughts in the Depths of the Galaxy II"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_2.r: "§g$ms_lit.maids_romance_2.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_2.d: "From that day on, the relationship between my master and me quietly changed.\nHis gaze was no longer as cold as it used to be; it had gained a touch of gentleness and warmth. Occasionally, when I was tidying up the room, he would stand nearby, quietly watching me, as if admiring a painting. I was completely flustered by this change; my heart pounded like a drum, and each time I would lower my head to hide my nervousness.\n\nOne day, in an unprecedented move, he invited me to the bridge. It was a place a maid had never set foot in before—the nerve center of the Imperial Fleet.\n“Lia, come here.” ” His voice was still deep, but now carried an added touch of tenderness. \nI walked over timidly, gazing at the dazzling Milky Way through the massive stargazing window. He stood before the window, his silhouette tall and slender, like a statue carved from starlight. \n“Leia,” he turned around, his gaze so gentle I could barely meet it, “what do you think of the stars?”\nI paused for a moment, then replied, “They’re… beautiful.” nHe gave a soft chuckle, a smile as gentle as a ray of morning light from the depths of the universe. “You’re even more beautiful than they are.”\nI was stunned by his words; my cheeks flushed instantly, and I didn’t even dare to lift my gaze. My Master’s footsteps drew closer, and I sensed him standing beside me.\n“Lia, do you know,” his voice was as soft as a whisper, “that ever since I met you, I’ve realized that even these starry skies have taken on meaning.” n\nMy heart was pounding so hard it felt like it would leap out of my chest, and the heat on my face was uncontrollable. I lifted my head and met his gentle gaze; it was as if the entire world had fallen silent in that instant.\n“Master…” My voice was as soft as a mosquito’s buzz, yet he heard it clearly. \nHe reached out and gently tucked a stray strand of my hair behind my ear, his touch so tender it felt as if I were in a dream. “Don’t call me ‘Master’ anymore,” he said softly. “Call me by my name.”\nI froze. Ever since I became his maid, this seemed to be the first time he’d asked me to do this. Instinctively, I murmured, “But, I…”\nHe reached out and took my hand, his tone firm yet gentle: “Lia, from now on, you’re not just my maid. I want you to be… my partner.”\nThose words streaked across the sea of my heart like a shooting star, leaving behind a brilliant, warm afterglow. I stared at him blankly, my mind a blank slate. Finally, I nodded gently, tears welling up in my eyes. \n\nHis smile was like the most illustrious light in the Milky Way. He reached out and pointed to the endless starry sky outside the window: “Lia, would you like to go there and take a look? From now on, I want to journey through the sea of stars with you and witness every bit of beauty this universe has to offer.” nWithout hesitation, I replied softly, “I’d love to.”\nFrom that day on, my identity changed; I was no longer just his nameless shadow. I became his companion across the starry sky, and under this starry canopy, a new chapter in our story quietly began.\n"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_3.t: "A Maid's Thoughts in the Depths of the Galaxy III"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_3.r: "§g$ms_lit.maids_romance_3.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.maids_romance_3.d: "Under the Empire’s illustrious glory, the Master’s name has long since become legendary. He led his fleet to sweep across the stars, defeating one hostile force after another, and was just one step away from the great feat of unifying the galaxy. And I, Lya, as his companion, witnessed it all with my own eyes. From our first encounter in the kitchen to later gazing upon the starry skies together, our story seemed destined to leave its mark on this vast universe.\nYet, just as the Empire was closest to victory, he mysteriously vanished. \n\nI remember that day clearly. It was the last time my master stood on the bridge; his posture was as upright as ever, yet his gaze seemed to carry a certain indescribable melancholy. He whispered to me, “Leia, if one day I’m gone, you must remember… at the end of the galaxy, there is always an answer.” nI didn’t have time to ask him what he meant by that. He turned and left the bridge, and soon after, his whereabouts seemed to be swallowed up by the cosmos—we heard nothing more from him. The fleet searched every possible area but found no trace of him. The entire Empire was thrown into chaos, and I, too, was enveloped by a deep sense of emptiness. \nBut I knew he must still be alive.\n\nMany in the maid corps shared my conviction. Though they were just ordinary maids, they all possessed loyal hearts. We embarked on an unprecedented search. Whether on abandoned planets, through asteroid belts, or at the perilous edges of black holes, we left our footprints everywhere. Whenever we found even the tiniest, most insignificant clue, the flame of hope would burn even brighter. \nOnce, on a desolate planet, we discovered the wreckage of a small spacecraft bearing his mark. I was almost moved to tears with joy, but the extent of the damage and the recorded data suggested he might have been caught up in some deeper conspiracy or unknown interstellar force. This only strengthened my resolve: no matter the cost, I would find him. \n\nOne year, two years… Time slipped quietly by amid the darkness and starlight of the cosmos. Every journey was fraught with danger, but I never gave up. This was not only for love, but also to honor that promise made beneath the warm starry sky. \n\nLater, the Empire’s maids formed several “Expeditions to Find the Master.” This force, composed of his once most loyal subordinates and equipped with the most advanced ships and detection equipment, had but one goal—to find the Master, to bring back the one who could restore order and hope to the galaxy. And I, without hesitation, joined them.\nIn the moment before the expeditionary force’s flagship lifted off, I stood by the porthole, looking down at the Imperial capital below. The maids came one after another to see me off; some even wiped away tears in secret. They knew this farewell might be forever. But I simply smiled to comfort them: “He must be waiting for us. I will find him and tell him that we never gave up.”\n\nAs the flagship slowly departed orbit, my gaze drifted through the porthole into the depths of the starry sea. In the vastness of the cosmos, countless unknowns await us, but the one thing I know for certain is that no matter how far or how long it takes, my journey has only one goal—to find him and tell him that I still remember our promise at the end of the galaxy.\n"
+  ms_lit.master_worship_1.t: "The master used a pebble to knock down the colossus of the Fallen Empire"
+  ms_lit.master_worship_1.r: "§g$ms_lit.master_worship_1.t$§!"
+  ms_lit.master_worship_1.d: "In the vast and boundless Interstellar Empire, every maid harbors deep reverence and loyalty toward her master. In their eyes, the master is not only the embodiment of power, but also a god-like being. He possesses immense Psionic Energy, capable not only of traversing the veil of time and space but also of commanding the trajectories of the stars. \n\nOne day, the evil, fallen empire dispatched a colossal colossus. This terrifying weapon—towering as high as a mountain and exuding an aura of destruction—was capable of obliterating an entire planet. A deep red glow radiated from the colossus’s core, and with every step it took, interstellar space trembled slightly. Faced with this threat, the Empire’s fleet was powerless, and the entire star system was plunged into a shroud of despair. \n\nHowever, just as the crisis reached its peak, the Master descended upon the battlefield. Standing at the command console of his flagship, his eyes radiated with extraordinary Radiance. He raised his hand, as if merging with the universe itself. A massive meteorite, long dormant at the edge of a distant galaxy, awoke. Driven by the Master’s power, it traversed the endless void and hurtled toward the Colossus. \n\nThe commanders of the evil empire fought with all their might to defend against it, but the meteor’s destructive force was unstoppable. It struck the Colossus with thunderous force, unleashing a shockwave powerful enough to light up a galaxy. Under the meteor’s impact, the colossus was reduced to cosmic dust, vanishing into thin air along with the Fallen Empire’s ambitions. \n\nThe maids watching from the rear of the battlefield witnessed this scene and fell to their knees, their eyes brimming with tears of emotion. “My Master truly is the guardian deity of the universe!” whispered a maid named Lina, her voice filled with reverence and awe. \n\nFrom that day forward, the legend of how the Master single-handedly destroyed the Colossus and saved the Interstellar Empire became a tale the maids sang of day after day. They firmly believed that as long as the Master was there, no enemy—no matter how powerful—could ever shake this great empire.\n"
+  ms_lit.master_worship_1.tt: "§L A passage found in the Dominator’s discarded terminal; all maids read and memorized the entire text while attending school.§!"
+  ms_lit.iron_heart_maid.t: "The Iron-Willed Maid"
